Key Functions of a Servo Motor1. Pan/Tilt Scanning (Directional Sensing)Instead of fixing the ultrasonic sensor pointing only straight ahead, mounting it on a servo allows the robot to rotate the sensor left and right (typically from $0^\circ$ to $180^\circ$) to scan its surroundings while remaining stationary.2. Decision-Making Before MovingWhen an obstacle is detected straight ahead:The robot stops.The servo rotates the sensor to the left (e.g., $180^\circ$) to measure distance.The servo rotates the sensor to the right (e.g., $0^\circ$) to measure distance.The robot compares both readings and turns toward whichever side has more clear space, rather than turning blindly.3. Wide Field-of-View (FOV)The ultrasonic sensor itself has a narrow detection cone ($< 15^\circ$). Sweeping the sensor back and forth with a servo expands the robot's detection zone up to $180^\circ$, preventing it from bumping into side obstacles or angled walls.  4. Dynamic Obstacle AvoidanceIt allows for advanced navigation algorithms—such as mapping nearby distance values into an array to find the optimal path out of tight corners or dead ends.Wiring Preview (Servo to Arduino)Servo WireSignal / PowerArduino PinOrange / YellowSignal / PWMDigital Pin (e.g., Pin 11)RedPower ($5\text{V}$)$5\text{V}$ (or external $5\text{V}$ supply)Brown / BlackGroundGND
